From 6a751e9992c558d4df84939938318c5fb6b33a80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: schoeremy <jeremyfroeb2007@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 17:48:07 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 61/64] nxhost: NX_NEXTENDO_INSPECT, a local relay that decodes
 NPLN traffic

Splatoon 3 speaks gRPC through its own BoringSSL, so a feature that stays
"offline" after the NPLN login gives no clue which call failed. With
NX_NEXTENDO_INSPECT=1 (Nextendo mode, macOS) connects to the Nextendo server
on 443 go to a relay on 127.0.0.1. It finishes TLS with the game, opens its
own TLS connection to the server with the game's SNI and ALPN h2, passes
every byte through unchanged and logs the HTTP/2 frames: request paths and
headers, response status, gRPC status and message, RST_STREAM, GOAWAY and
readable strings in the payloads ("[npln] ..."). Header values and strings
that look like credentials are logged by length only.

gRPC checks the server's host name after the handshake (s3certbypass only
skips chain verification), so the relay's throwaway certificate carries the
NPLN host patterns as SANs; with a non-matching name the game sent the
preface and gave up (2321-5760). The certificate and key protect nothing:
the relay only listens on localhost.

Tested in a localhost-only sandbox against a local TLS server: the game's
Auth/IssuePrearrangedUserToken call is decoded, its id token hidden.
